@lesson adversarial-examples
Q: What is the FGSM perturbation?
* \(\epsilon\cdot\mathrm{sign}(\nabla_{\mathbf x}\mathcal L)\)
- \(-\epsilon\nabla_\theta\mathcal L\)
- Random Gaussian noise
- \(\epsilon\mathbf x\)
> One step in the input direction that increases the loss, scaled to an \(\ell_\infty\) budget.

Q: A linear model has \(\|\mathbf w\|_1=50\). By how much can an \(\ell_\infty\) perturbation with \(\epsilon=0.01\) change its score?
= 0.5 ±0.001
> \(\epsilon\|\mathbf w\|_1=0.5\). Small per-feature changes add up in high dimensions.

Q: Which defence has held up best against strong adaptive attacks?
* Adversarial training with PGD
- JPEG compression of inputs
- Hiding the gradients
- Adding a detector network
> Preprocessing and detection defences are frequently broken by attackers who know about them.

Q: Adversarial examples crafted on one model often fool other models trained on similar data.
= true
> Transferability enables black-box attacks through substitute models.

@lesson poisoning-privacy
Q: What makes a backdoor attack hard to notice with ordinary evaluation?
* The model behaves normally unless a specific trigger appears
- It lowers accuracy on every input
- It deletes the training data
- It only affects training speed
> Clean-test accuracy stays high, so dedicated scanning and trusted data are needed.

Q: A membership-inference attack reaches 0.52 accuracy. What does that suggest?
* Little leakage: close to random guessing
- Severe leakage
- The model is overfitting heavily
- The attack is perfect
> 0.5 is chance level for balanced member/non-member sets.

Q: Which practices reduce training-data memorization and leakage? (select all)
* Deduplicating training data
* Differentially private training
* Removing personal data before training
- Training many more epochs on the same data
> Overtraining increases memorization.

Q: In \((\epsilon,\delta)\)-differential privacy, a smaller \(\epsilon\) means…
* stronger privacy
- weaker privacy
- faster training
- no noise
> The model's behaviour can depend less on any one person's data.

@lesson llm-security
Q: An agent summarizes a web page that contains hidden text telling it to email the user's files. What is this?
* Indirect prompt injection
- A jailbreak by the user
- Data poisoning
- Model extraction
> The malicious instructions arrive through content the model reads, not from the user.

Q: Which controls belong outside the model? (select all)
* Tool permission scopes and recipient allowlists
* Human confirmation for irreversible actions
* Access control on which documents can be retrieved
- Asking the model in the system prompt to be careful
> Prompts help but cannot be the guarantee.

Q: Why is rendering arbitrary markdown images from model output risky?
* An injected image URL can carry private data to an attacker's server when it loads
- Images slow down the page
- Markdown is insecure by design
- It costs more tokens
> Image fetching is a classic exfiltration channel.

Q: Model output passed into a SQL query or shell should be treated as untrusted input.
= true
> Otherwise prompt injection becomes SQL injection or remote code execution.

@lesson ai-governance
Q: Which document states a model's intended use, out-of-scope uses, evaluation results by subgroup and known limitations?
* A model card
- A README for the API
- A changelog
- A license
> Datasheets do the same for datasets; system cards for whole LLM applications.

Q: Which of these systems typically needs the heaviest controls?
- A playlist recommender
- An internal code-search tool
* A CV-screening model used in hiring
- A spell checker
> It affects access to jobs, can encode discrimination, and is commonly treated as high-risk.

Q: The EU AI Act takes a risk-based approach with different obligations for different categories of systems.
= true
> Some practices are prohibited, high-risk uses carry heavy obligations, and others have transparency duties.

Q: Which elements belong in a pre-launch risk assessment? (select all)
* Who is affected, including non-users
* How harmful each kind of error is
* Mitigations with owners
* Go / no-go metrics decided in advance
> Write it down before building and revisit it before launch.

@lesson exam
Q: A content filter blocks toxic comments, but attackers bypass it with misspellings and invisible Unicode characters. What is the best response? (select all)
* Normalize text (Unicode, homoglyphs) before classifying
* Add adversarial examples to training and evaluation
* Keep human review for borderline cases
- Rely on the original filter and ignore the bypasses
> Attackers adapt; defence needs normalization, adversarial data and humans in the loop.

Q: You download a pretrained model checkpoint from an unknown source. What should you prefer?
* A safetensors file from a verified source with a checked hash
- A pickle file from any mirror
- Running it on your laptop with all credentials loaded
- Ignoring the file format
> Pickle-based checkpoints can execute code on load.

Q: Which statement about prompt injection is accurate?
* No known filter blocks it completely, so systems must limit what a successful injection can do
- A strong system prompt prevents it entirely
- It only affects small models
- It requires access to model weights
> Defence in depth is the realistic goal.

Q: A team trains with DP-SGD and accuracy drops most for a rare subgroup. Why is this expected?
* Noise and clipping hurt most where there are few examples to average over
- DP-SGD ignores subgroups
- The subgroup has more data
- DP always improves accuracy
> Privacy noise is relatively larger for small groups.
